---
import InstallBlock from "./InstallBlock.astro";
import TerminalDemo from "./TerminalDemo.astro";
import Backers from "./Backers.astro";
import { quickstart, links, pkg } from "../config";
import { stats, compact } from "../stats";
import { postPath } from "../blog";

// Two-column hero, with a backer strip across the foot of the fold:
//
//   ┌──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────┐
//   │ chip                     │                           │
//   │ headline                 │   terminal                │
//   │ subhead                  │                           │
//   │ buttons + install bar    │   stats                   │
//   ├──────────────────────────┴───────────────────────────┤
//   │            BACKED BY   ← logos rolling →             │
//   └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
//
// It was a centred single column in the supermemory.ai shape before this, and
// the measurements below are still theirs — the type sizes, the 33px chip, the
// 194+12+194 action column. What changed is the axis: side by side, the fold
// has to seat the *taller* of the two columns rather than the sum of the whole
// stack, which is what buys the room the marquee now spends. Which column that
// is varies: the terminal used to be taller everywhere, and since the headline
// came up a step and the stage went to 90% the two run within a few px of each
// other on most desktops. That slack, not the fold, is what the headline and
// subhead are sized against — see h1, where the measurements are.
//
// The stats line stays where it was in reading order — directly under the
// transcript, as a caption on the claim rather than a line to cross to reach
// it. Each stat renders only when its build-time fetch succeeded (stats.ts),
// so the row disappears cleanly until the repo and package are public.
//
// The product render and its "Proven on real copper" caption used to hold the
// right-hand column here; the terminal has it now, and they live in
// Proof.astro, the section immediately below.
const showStats = stats.stars !== null || stats.downloads !== null;
---

<section id="top" class="hero">
  <!-- Routed copper. See the tile's construction in the frontmatter above;
       traces cross because on a two-layer board they are on different layers. -->
  <div class="grid-dots" aria-hidden="true"></div>

  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="split">
      <div class="copy">
        <a class="chip" href={postPath("from-brief-to-gerbers")}>
          <span class="chip-tag"
            ><span class="chip-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span>New</span
          >
          <span class="chip-text"
            >From brief.md to gerbers<svg
              class="chip-arrow"
              viewBox="0 0 16 16"
              width="12"
              height="12"
              aria-hidden="true"
              focusable="false"
              ><path
                fill="none"
                stroke="currentColor"
                stroke-width="1.6"
                stroke-linecap="round"
                stroke-linejoin="round"
                d="M3 8h9.5M9 4.5 12.5 8 9 11.5"></path></svg
            ></span
          >
        </a>

        <h1>Cursor for circuit boards<span class="stop">.</span></h1>

        {
          /* Length is set by how this wraps on a phone, and the lever is the words:
        no type size fixes it. The original ran to four lines at 375 and three
        at 390, and reached two only at 9.5-11.5px against 15px body, which is
        why "Built for real hardware" went rather than the font shrinking.
        As it stands this is three lines from 360 to 430 and two on desktop.
        Two on phones as well needs one more cut — dropping "is an AI PCB
        engineer" does it, at the cost of the category label for a first-time
        reader. Measured, not guessed; re-measure before trading it away. */
        }
        <p class="sub">
          Copperhead is an open-source AI engineering platform that helps
          hardware teams design, verify and ship circuit boards.
        </p>

        <div class="cta">
          <div class="actions">
            {/* Routes to the hosted platform, not down the page to
                #quickstart. That anchor is still the footer's "Quickstart"
                target and the CLI tier's "Get started" on /pricing, so the
                block it points at keeps both of its inbound links. */}
            <a class="primary" href={links.app}>
              <span class="label">Start building</span>
              <span class="tip"
                ><svg
                  viewBox="0 0 16 16"
                  width="14"
                  height="14"
                  aria-hidden="true"
                  focusable="false"
                  ><path
                    fill="none"
                    stroke="currentColor"
                    stroke-width="1.7"
                    stroke-linecap="round"
                    stroke-linejoin="round"
                    d="M3 8h9.5M9 4.5 12.5 8 9 11.5"></path></svg
                ></span
              >
            </a>
            <!-- The ↗ came across with the label from the tertiary link this
             replaced: docs live off-site, and the outbound arrow is the one
             thing the button pair does not otherwise say. Drawn rather than set
             as a glyph because ↗ is not in the font subsets and fell back to a
             face that did not match the page. -->
            <a class="secondary" href={links.docs}>
              Read the docs<svg
                class="arrow"
                viewBox="0 0 16 16"
                width="12"
                height="12"
                aria-hidden="true"
                focusable="false"
                ><path
                  fill="none"
                  stroke="currentColor"
                  stroke-width="1.6"
                  stroke-linecap="round"
                  stroke-linejoin="round"
                  d="M4 12 12 4M6.5 4H12v5.5"></path></svg
              >
            </a>
          </div>

          <div class="install">
            <!-- the install line only; the key + create steps stay in the full
             quickstart block this button scrolls to -->
            <InstallBlock lines={[quickstart[0]]} bar />
          </div>
          <!-- The dashed tertiary link that used to close this block was also "Read
           the docs", so promoting it into the button pair left the two stacked
           one above the other. The link is the one that goes: the button says
           the same thing in the place the eye reaches first, and dropping it
           takes ~48px off the fold. "Let's talk" still has its home in the
           navbar CTA and again at the foot of the page. -->
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- The right column: the transcript, with its receipts under it. It used
           to sit below the copy and be cropped by the fold, which is where the
           "there is more" cue came from. Beside the copy it fits whole, so
           nothing is left half-visible to invite the scroll; the backer strip
           at the foot of the fold does that job now. -->
      <div class="stage">
        <div class="demo">
          <TerminalDemo />
        </div>

        {
          showStats && (
            <div class="proof">
              <p class="stats" role="status">
                {stats.stars !== null && (
                  <a
                    class="stat"
                    href={links.repo}
                    aria-label={`${stats.stars} stars on GitHub`}
                  >
                    <svg
                      class="ico"
                      viewBox="0 0 16 16"
                      width="14"
                      height="14"
                      aria-hidden="true"
                      focusable="false"
                    >
                      <path
                        fill="currentColor"
                        d="M8 .5l2.3 4.66 5.15.75-3.72 3.63.88 5.13L8 12.34l-4.6 2.42.88-5.13L.55 5.9l5.15-.75L8 .5z"
                      />
                    </svg>
                    <strong class="num">{compact(stats.stars)}</strong>
                    <span class="lbl">GitHub stars</span>
                  </a>
                )}
                {stats.stars !== null && stats.downloads !== null && (
                  <span class="sep" aria-hidden="true" />
                )}
                {stats.downloads !== null && (
                  <a
                    class="stat"
                    href={`https://www.npmjs.com/package/${pkg}`}
                    aria-label={`${stats.downloads} installs a month on npm`}
                  >
                    <strong class="num">{compact(stats.downloads)}</strong>
                    <span class="lbl">installs / month</span>
                  </a>
                )}
              </p>
            </div>
          )
        }
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- The foot of the fold. Outside .wrap, so the rail runs the full --maxw box
       and its ends feather against the frame's vertical rules rather than
       stopping 32px inside them. `flex: none` keeps it out of the fight for the
       fold's slack: the column above absorbs a short viewport, this does not. -->
  <div class="strip">
    <Backers caption="Backed by" />
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  /* Centred hero filling the first viewport (svh so mobile browser chrome does
     not push the fold off-screen). A fixed height, not a floor: the section *is*
     the fold: exactly one viewport tall on any screen with the room, which from
     1080p up is every screen. A floor rather than a fixed height, because the
     terminal is never cropped (see .demo) — on a window too short to seat the
     whole stack the section grows and the stats row slides below the fold
     instead, still on the page and one scroll away.
     A column, not a row: the split takes the slack and the backer strip keeps
     its own height, so a short window eats into the gap around the copy rather
     than into the logos. */
  .hero {
    position: relative;
    min-height: 100svh;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    /* Written as "clear the bar, then leave a gap" rather than as one number,
       because the bar is fixed and the hero cannot see it: any padding
       expressed as a bare svh fraction shrinks toward the bar on a short
       viewport and the chip ends up crowding it. Only the gap term scales, and
       its floor is 30px — the tightest that still reads as air under the rule
       rather than as the chip resting on it. --nav-h so the reserved space
       tracks the bar's own height instead of a copy of it.

       The bottom term is the one lever on where the backer strip sits. The
       split centres in whatever height is left above the strip, so a pixel
       added here lifts the strip by a pixel and closes the gap above it by
       half — which is why the strip rides higher than the old 4svh/56 left it
       without the copy beside it having to move much. It stays stated in svh
       and capped so the lift tracks the room available rather than spending a
       flat number that a short window does not have.

       9svh, measured rather than reasoned about. An earlier note here called
       7svh the ceiling on the grounds that 1280x720 had only ~64px of slack to
       give back — that was true of the layout before this one, and is not true
       of this one. At 9svh the numbers are: 720 spends 64.8px and the split
       still holds 42px it is not using, with the terminal ending at 505 of 720;
       945 spends 85px against 225px of slack; 1080 spends 97.2px against 259px.
       The terminal is in the fold at every one of them, and 720 is the tightest
       by a wide margin.

       Re-measure before moving it again, and measure the split's spare height
       rather than the terminal's bottom edge — the terminal has hundreds of
       pixels of clearance at 720 and stops being the thing that binds long
       before the copy column does. */
    padding-block: calc(var(--nav-h) + clamp(30px, 4.6svh, 58px))
      clamp(28px, 9svh, 104px);
    overflow: hidden;
  }

  /* A column flex container so the stack can centre itself in the fold on a
     screen with slack — `safe center` rather than plain `center` so that when
     the stack is instead taller than the fold it falls back to top-anchored,
     which keeps the headline on screen rather than pushing it off the top.
     Nothing here shrinks. Everything has a natural height it wants and gets it
     at every viewport, including the terminal: on a window too short for the
     lot, the column simply runs past the fold and the stats row is what ends up
     below it. That is the intended order of sacrifice — the transcript is the
     product and has to be legible; the stats are a caption on it and read just
     as well one scroll down. */
  .hero .wrap {
    position: relative;
    /* takes every pixel the strip does not, and centres the split inside it */
    flex: 1;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    justify-content: safe center;
  }

  .hero .wrap > * {
    flex: none;
  }

  /* ── The hero, and only the hero, runs wider than the content column. It sits
     outside .framed by design (see the frame note in global.css: it is the
     page's cover, not a cell of the grid), so there are no vertical rules for
     its edges to miss and the wider box costs nothing in alignment.

     Gated on viewport *height*, which looks wrong and is the whole point. The
     transcript is the thing that has to fill the right track, and its size is
     set by TerminalDemo's `(min-width: 901px) and (max-height: 1000px)` step:
     at 12px it caps at 733px wide, at the 10px that step drops it to, 611px.
     A 1080p monitor lands on the short side of that — a maximised browser
     there gives ~945-980px of viewport, not 1080 — so on the most common
     desktop the window is 611 and the track already has ~15px it is not using.
     Widen the box there and every pixel goes into that gap rather than into
     the terminal: measured, the slack runs 15px → 73px at 1440x900 and 89px at
     1920x960, and since the window is `margin: 0 auto` it shows up as air on
     both sides of the transcript with the two columns drifting apart.

     Above 1000px tall the terminal is at its 733 size and grows straight into
     the wider track instead — measured at 0px of slack at 1440x1040, 1920x1100
     and 2560x1300. So the condition here is the complement of the terminal's
     own step, and the two move together by construction: change that step's
     height threshold and this one has to follow it.

     The backer strip is deliberately not on this. It stays at --maxw so its
     feathered ends still land on the frame rules that begin immediately below
     the hero. ── */
  @media (min-height: 1001px) {
    .hero .wrap {
      max-width: var(--maxw-hero);
    }
  }

  /* ── The two columns. `minmax(0, …)` on both tracks rather than a bare
     fraction: a grid track's default minimum is auto, and the terminal's
     longest tool line would hold the right track open at its own min-content
     width, pushing the copy narrower than its share and eventually overflowing
     the page. The fractions are uneven because the terminal is the wider
     object and wants the room: at --maxw it comes out near 600/648, which
     leaves the transcript just inside its own 733px cap, so it fills its
     column and needs no centring.
     `center` rather than `start` so the shorter column sits against the middle
     of the taller one — the copy is the shorter one at every desktop width, and
     top-aligning it left the whole left half looking like it had slipped. ── */
  .split {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 0.92fr) minmax(0, 1fr);
    align-items: center;
    gap: clamp(2rem, 4.4vw, 4.5rem);
  }

  /* The hero used to centre everything; it now reads left, in both columns and
     at every width. Stated once here rather than left to each child, which is
     what the `margin-inline: auto` on the headline and subhead used to be
     fighting. */
  .copy,
  .stage {
    min-width: 0;
    text-align: left;
  }

  /* ── The two columns against each other. They are centred: `align-items:
     center` on .split, which is the base rule, so each column is its own
     natural height and the two share a centre line.

     This was `stretch` for a while, with the copy column distributing itself
     over the row and the stage pinned top and bottom, because centring shows a
     height difference twice — half of it as an inset at the top and half again
     at the bottom — and at the time the difference was 76px at 1920x1080. Two
     things have since taken most of that away: the headline and subhead came up
     a step, and the stage went to 90% below. Measured now, the two columns are
     within a few px of each other at 1280x720 and at 1920 and up, and the worst
     case left is ~35px at 1512x945 and 1600x900 — small enough that centring
     reads as balance rather than as the chip having slipped.

     Centring is also what keeps .proof's margin honest. Distributing the stage
     put the slack into the one gap between the window and the stats, which is a
     caption distance and should not be elastic: it measured 60.5px at 1512x945
     against the 22.7px the margin asks for, and near the margin at 1280 and
     1920, so the same relationship was drawn three different ways depending on
     the window. Natural height means the gap is the margin, everywhere. ── */
  @media (min-width: 1081px) {
    /* The scale knob for the right-hand column, and the only thing this query
       does. `zoom` and not `max-height`: a height cap does shrink the box —
       measured, 424 to 382 at 1280x720 — but it shrinks nothing inside it. The
       window stays 604x382 and the stats row is simply cut off below the new
       edge, which is a crop, not a smaller terminal; `overflow: hidden` only
       makes the crop deliberate. `zoom` scales the box and its contents
       together, and unlike `transform: scale()` it does it in layout, so the
       grid row comes down with it and the column beside it sees the new height.
       The window goes 604x382 -> 550x344 and 700x458 -> 660x412, both at
       1.60:1, the ratio it already had, and no type size in TerminalDemo.astro
       is touched.

       Scoped to the width where there are two columns at all — below 1081
       .split is a single track and the terminal is the full-size object again. */
    .stage {
      zoom: 0.9;
    }
  }

  /* The strip's own clearance from the split above it. Note that this does not
     move the strip — its top is pinned by the hero's bottom padding and its own
     height — it moves the split *down* into the slack this gives back, which is
     the other half of closing the gap between the receipts and the logos. Kept
     large enough that the stacked layout, where this is all that parts the
     terminal from the strip, still reads as a break. */
  .strip {
    flex: none;
    margin-top: clamp(12px, 2.6svh, 2rem);
  }

  /* A diamond lattice, off supermemory's field. Theirs is measured —

       y=396  x = 26.5, 38.5, 50.5, 62.5 …
       y=402  x = 32.5, 56.5 …

     — two square grids on a 12px pitch, the second offset by (6,6). That is a
     quincunx, and it does put every dot's nearest neighbours on its diagonals.
     But its cell is a square standing on its corner, and the eye does not read
     a square on point as a diamond: at 12/12 the diagonals are equal, the
     verticals and horizontals stay legible as rows and columns, and the field
     reads as offset rows.

     So the cell is a rhombus rather than a square: --pitch across, --rise down,
     and the second lattice at half of each. Unequal diagonals are what make the
     shape read — 16 wide on 11 tall is a 1.45 ratio, past the point where the
     eye stops resolving the orthogonals and follows the diagonals instead.

     The pitch is also wider than theirs, which is the other half of matching
     the reference. Two lattices at 12px put a dot every 72px² — twice the
     density of the field being matched, which is what made this read as texture
     rather than as a lattice. 16x11 comes to one every 88px², near their ~121,
     and the dot shrinks with it: 0.65px of solid ramping out to 1.15px, for a
     2.3px mark where this used to set a 3.4px one. Fineness is most of what
     separates a lattice from a texture — at the old diameter the diamonds fill
     in and the field closes up into tone.

     That ramp is not softness for its own sake. A hard-edged radial gradient
     gets no antialiasing, and at this diameter the rasteriser resolves each dot
     as a square — which on a diamond lattice is the one artefact that would
     give the whole construction away. Their ink renders at rgb(166,222,255) on
     white, a tint rather than a solid, which 38% copper matches for distance
     from the page in either theme. */
  .grid-dots {
    --pitch: 16px;
    --rise: 11px;
    /* the ramp's outer edge; the gradient is sized to it so the dot is not
       clipped by its own tile */
    --edge: 1.15px;

    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    pointer-events: none;
    background-image:
      radial-gradient(
        circle at var(--edge) var(--edge),
        color-mix(in srgb, var(--copper) 38%, transparent) 0 0.65px,
        transparent var(--edge)
      ),
      radial-gradient(
        circle at var(--edge) var(--edge),
        color-mix(in srgb, var(--copper) 38%, transparent) 0 0.65px,
        transparent var(--edge)
      );
    background-size:
      var(--pitch) var(--rise),
      var(--pitch) var(--rise);
    background-position:
      0 0,
      calc(var(--pitch) / 2) calc(var(--rise) / 2);
    /* The field is a frame: dense along all four edges, thinning inward to
       almost nothing through the middle.

       Two gradients, one per axis, and they are unioned rather than
       intersected — that is the whole thing. `intersect` multiplies, so a pixel
       had to be near the left/right edge *and* low on the page to survive, which
       is why every earlier version was a pair of side bands or a dome rather
       than a border. `add` takes the stronger of the two, so a pixel is dense
       if it is near any edge, the corners get both, and the centre keeps only
       what the two flat middles sum to.

       Those middles are 3% each, so the centre sits at 6% — enough that the
       copy column is not a bare white hole in the field, low enough to read
       every line of type over it.

       The four edges are not equal. Left, right and bottom run to full, but the
       top peaks at 14% and is back to the flat middle by a fifth of the way
       down: it is the edge nearest the navbar's rule and the one the headline
       sits closest to. At full strength it read as a second rule under the
       first, and even at 30% over a band 350px deep it put a wash across the
       whole headline. The corners keep their weight regardless — the
       horizontal gradient is already at full there, and under `add` the
       stronger of the two wins — so the frame still closes at all four corners
       rather than fraying at the top. The transparent sliver above it keeps the
       field off the navbar rule entirely. */
    -webkit-mask-image:
      linear-gradient(
        90deg,
        #000 0%,
        rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.03) 30%,
        rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.03) 70%,
        #000 100%
      ),
      linear-gradient(
        180deg,
        transparent 0%,
        rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.14) 4%,
        rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.03) 20%,
        rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.03) 74%,
        #000 100%
      );
    -webkit-mask-composite: source-over;
    mask-image:
      linear-gradient(
        90deg,
        #000 0%,
        rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.03) 30%,
        rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.03) 70%,
        #000 100%
      ),
      linear-gradient(
        180deg,
        transparent 0%,
        rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.14) 4%,
        rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.03) 20%,
        rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.03) 74%,
        #000 100%
      );
    mask-composite: add;
  }

  /* ── Announcement chip. Measured off supermemory's: 33px tall on a panel
     fill, a 1px accent-tinted frame, and two 6px/10px segments. There is no
     rule between them — the tag's tinted fill ends and that edge reads as the
     divider. ── */
  .chip {
    display: inline-flex;
    /* It sat in a centred column flex before and needed align-self to centre
       itself; inside .copy it is an inline-level box in a left-set block and
       simply starts where the text does. */
    align-items: stretch;
    height: 33px;
    border: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--copper) 25%, transparent);
    background: var(--panel);
    font-size: 0.78125rem; /* 12.5px */
    font-weight: 500;
    letter-spacing: -0.005em;
    text-decoration: none;
    transition: border-color 0.16s ease;
  }

  .chip:hover {
    border-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--copper) 45%, transparent);
  }

  .chip-tag {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 6px;
    padding: 6px 10px;
    background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--copper) 8%, transparent);
    color: var(--copper);
  }

  /* A 12px outlined square with an 8px filled one inside it, drawn from one
     element: the 1px border and 1px padding inset the content box to 8px, and
     background-clip keeps the fill off the padding. */
  .chip-dot {
    width: 12px;
    height: 12px;
    flex: none;
    padding: 1px;
    border: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--copper) 40%, transparent);
    background: var(--copper);
    background-clip: content-box;
  }

  .chip-text {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 8px;
    padding: 6px 10px;
    color: var(--text);
  }

  .chip-arrow {
    flex: none;
    opacity: 0.6;
  }

  /* ── Headline. supermemory's metrics are the shape — Space Grotesk 500,
     74.88px leading on 72px (1.04), -4.176px of tracking (-0.058em) — but not
     the size any more: this runs a step larger than theirs on a desktop, and
     the leading and tracking are ratios so they came up with it.
     The `min()` adds a height term to the usual width-driven clamp, because a
     headline is the one item on the fold that can eat the terminal's room. What
     makes the larger step affordable is that it does not: the terminal column
     is the taller of the two at every desktop width — measured, 424 against a
     296px copy column at 1280x720 and 510 against 403 at 1920x1080 — so the
     copy has 40-128px of slack before it starts setting the height of the
     split at all. The numbers below spend that slack and stop inside it; the
     tightest is 1512x945, where the copy column has 40px to give. ── */
  h1 {
    /* Both terms are up a step from the 4.4vw/7svh this ran before. Measured
       across the desktop range, the pair lands at 60px at 1280x720, 67px at
       1440x800, 79px at 1512x945 and the 5.25rem ceiling from 1920 up — where
       it was 50/56/66/72. The wrap is unchanged by it: `balance` sets this as
       "Cursor for" over "circuit boards." at every desktop width, and the
       second line is the longer one, so the test when moving these is whether
       "circuit boards." still fits the copy track (it wants ~6.4em, against a
       556-644px column). Re-measure at 1280, 1440, 1512 and 1920.
       The ceiling exists to stop the headline running away on a wall display;
       nothing below 2560 reaches it through the width term, so raising it only
       moves the tall-and-wide screens, which is exactly where 72px had started
       to read small against the terminal beside it. */
    font-size: clamp(2.25rem, min(5.2vw, 8.4svh), 5.25rem);
    font-weight: 500;
    line-height: 1.04;
    letter-spacing: -0.058em;
    /* The column is the measure now, so the 18ch cap has nothing left to do on
       a desktop; it stays as the cap for the stacked layout, where the copy
       runs the full width. */
    max-width: 18ch;
    margin: clamp(18px, 3.4svh, 28px) 0 0;
    text-wrap: balance;
  }

  /* supermemory ends its headline on a coloured full stop; copper, not blue */
  .stop {
    color: var(--copper);
  }

  /* Was 15px on 24px leading in a 600px measure, supermemory's subhead exactly.
     It reads thin under the larger headline above, so it takes the same step
     up: 17px on the same 1.6 leading, and the measure widens with it, because
     600px was 40em of text and is only 35em at 17px. At 640px this is two
     lines from 1150px of viewport up; below that the copy track is narrower
     than the cap and sets the measure itself, and it runs to three lines for
     the ~70px of width between the split's 1081px floor and 1150 — which the
     copy column has the height for (403 against the stage's 430 at 1081x900).
     Phones are unaffected: the <=720px block below restates 15px. */
  .sub {
    color: var(--text-dim);
    font-size: 1.0625rem; /* 17px */
    line-height: 1.6;
    letter-spacing: -0.012em;
    max-width: 640px;
    margin: clamp(12px, 2.4svh, 20px) 0 0;
    /* balance, not pretty: pretty only rescues a single trailing word, and this
       sentence was breaking to leave "a prompt." alone on the second line */
    text-wrap: balance;
  }

  /* ── Action column. The button pair and the install bar share one width, which
     is the detail that makes this block read as a single unit: 194 + 12 + 194
     comes to the 400px the bar and the tertiary link both take. ── */
  .cta {
    width: min(100%, 400px);
    margin: clamp(20px, 4svh, 32px) 0 0;
  }

  /* Grid, not flex: against `flex-basis: 0` the secondary's 20px side padding
     and 1px border are added on top of its computed share rather than fitted
     inside it, so the pair came out 173/215 instead of an even split. Grid
     tracks size the border box, which is what makes both halves exactly 194. */
  .actions {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    gap: 12px;
  }

  /* Primary splits into a 20px-padded label and a 44px arrow cap, parted by a
     rule struck through the fill itself. 44px tall on 14.5px medium. */
  .primary {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: stretch;
    height: 44px;
    overflow: hidden;
    background: var(--copper);
    color: var(--on-copper);
    font-size: 0.90625rem; /* 14.5px */
    font-weight: 500;
    letter-spacing: -0.005em;
    text-decoration: none;
    transition: background-color 0.16s ease;
  }

  .primary:hover {
    background: var(--copper-hi);
    color: var(--on-copper);
  }

  .primary .label {
    flex: 1;
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    padding: 0 20px;
  }

  /* Struck in the label's own colour, so the rule survives the label going from
     white on copper to near-black on copper. */
  .primary .tip {
    flex: none;
    width: 44px;
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    border-left: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--on-copper) 24%, transparent);
  }

  .secondary {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    gap: 7px;
    height: 44px;
    padding: 0 20px;
    background: var(--panel);
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    color: var(--text);
    font-size: 0.90625rem; /* 14.5px */
    font-weight: 500;
    letter-spacing: -0.005em;
    text-decoration: none;
    transition:
      background-color 0.16s ease,
      border-color 0.16s ease;
  }

  .secondary:hover {
    background: var(--panel-hi);
    border-color: var(--line-hi);
    color: var(--text);
  }

  /* Dimmer than the label at rest so the button still reads as one word-shape
     rather than as text plus an icon; it comes up to full on hover, which is
     also when "this leaves the site" is the useful thing to say. */
  .secondary .arrow {
    flex: none;
    opacity: 0.55;
    transition: opacity 0.16s ease;
  }

  .secondary:hover .arrow {
    opacity: 1;
  }

  .primary:active,
  .secondary:active {
    scale: 0.99;
  }

  .install {
    margin-top: 12px;
    text-align: left;
  }

  /* ── Social proof, where supermemory runs a customer logo wall. The label
     that used to head this row is gone; the numbers speak without it. ── */
  /* The stats sit under the terminal, not above it: the transcript is the claim
     and these are its receipts, so they read as a caption on it rather than as
     a line the reader must cross to reach it. */
  /* The gap between the window and the receipts it captions. Trimmed from
     clamp(18px, 3svh, 32px): with the stage a flex column that ends on .proof,
     this margin is the last term in the column's height whenever the stage is
     the taller of the two — which it is at every desktop width — so it comes
     straight off the row and, with it, off the fold. The stage's own 0.9 scale
     is on top of this, so what actually renders on a desktop is 0.9 of these
     numbers again: ~23px at the ceiling rather than the 32 this started at. */
  .proof {
    margin-top: clamp(15px, 2.4svh, 26px);
  }

  /* The number carries the weight (dark, tabular) and the unit sits dim beside
     it, the two stats parted by a hairline rule rather than a floating dot. */
  .stats {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    /* Centred on the transcript above them, not on the copy beside it — see
       the note under .demo. */
    justify-content: center;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.5rem 1.15rem;
    /* the 20px here used to part the row from its label; .proof's own top
       margin is the only gap it needs now */
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: 0.86rem;
    color: var(--text-dim);
  }

  /* Each figure links to the thing it is counting — the star count to the repo,
     the install count to the package. Undecorated at rest so the row still reads
     as one quiet line of numbers; the page's dashed rule appears on hover. */
  .stat {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: baseline;
    gap: 0.45em;
    white-space: nowrap;
    color: inherit;
    text-decoration-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--copper) 45%, transparent);
    transition:
      color 0.16s ease,
      text-decoration-color 0.16s ease;
  }

  .stat:hover {
    color: var(--text);
    text-decoration-color: var(--copper);
  }

  .stat:hover .lbl {
    color: var(--text);
  }

  .stat .ico {
    flex: none;
    align-self: center;
    color: var(--copper);
  }

  .stat .num {
    color: var(--text);
    font-weight: 500;
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }

  .stat .lbl {
    color: var(--text-dim);
  }

  .stats .sep {
    width: 1px;
    height: 0.95em;
    align-self: center;
    background: var(--hairline);
  }

  /* The terminal breaks the copy column: the copy is capped near 600px and this
     runs to 900, which is what makes it read as the product rather than as
     another block of the page. text-align is reset because .hero .wrap centres
     everything, and a centred transcript is not a transcript. */
  /* 48, not the 72 this started at. The terminal is a different class of object
     from the copy above it and earns a bigger step than the 36px that parts the
     subhead from the buttons — but only a bit bigger. The link above it carries
     ~12px of its own padding and underline offset, so this lands at 60px of
     visible gap, and the 24px saved is 24px more terminal above the fold. */
  /* The one shrinkable item in the column, and the reason the fold holds. Given
     `0 1 auto` it never grows past the transcript's own height, but it absorbs
     the entire shortfall when the stack does not fit — 275px of it on a
     1280x720 window. The explicit `min-height` is load-bearing twice over: a
     flex item defaults to `min-height: auto`, which refuses to shrink below its
     content and would push the whole overrun back onto the stats row, and
     stating a real floor stops a very short window from cropping the transcript
     to a hairline. Below that floor the terminal is no longer worth showing and
     the stack overflows instead, top-anchored, exactly as it did before. */
  .demo {
    /* First in its own column, so nothing above it to clear. The margin comes
       back with the stacked layout, where the copy is above it again. */
    margin-top: 0;
    text-align: left;
  }

  /* The terminal keeps TerminalDemo's own `margin: 0 auto`, and the stats
     centre in the same track — so the two share a centre line and the receipts
     sit centred under the thing they are receipts for at every width. Worth
     stating because the alternative looks equivalent and is not: left-aligning
     both to the track start breaks wherever the terminal is held at its 611px
     cap inside a wider track (roughly 1400-1600), which puts its left edge
     ~7px right of the row beneath it. Centres cannot drift like that. */

  /* ── Where the two columns stop fitting. Below this the copy track drops
     under ~470px, at which point the headline breaks a word onto a line of its
     own and the transcript's 72-character tool lines start wrapping — so the
     split folds back into the stack it came from: copy, terminal, stats,
     logos, in reading order, which is the DOM order already.
     1080 rather than a round 1024 because a 1024 tablet is exactly the width
     where two columns technically fit and visibly should not. ── */
  @media (max-width: 1080px) {
    .split {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
      gap: 0;
    }

    /* The terminal has the copy above it again, so it needs the step back. */
    .demo {
      margin-top: clamp(16px, 3svh, 28px);
    }

    /* Centred, exactly as the hero was before the split — the left set is a
       property of having a column beside you, and stacked there is not one.
       The chip needs nothing here: it is inline-level, so it rides this.
       .install and the transcript assert their own left set and are unaffected,
       which is right — a centred command line is not a command line. */
    .copy,
    .stage {
      text-align: center;
    }

    h1,
    .sub,
    .cta {
      margin-inline: auto;
    }
  }

  /* The field used to be switched off here: with two vertical bands there was
     no width left either side of the copy for them to live in. A radial mask
     has no such problem — it thins wherever the copy is and keeps the corners —
     so the phone keeps its backdrop, on a tighter pitch and a step quieter. */
  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .grid-dots {
      opacity: 0.75;
    }
  }

  /* Phones get the same layout on tighter spacing. Every step above the fold
     closes up — the hero has to seat a headline, a subhead, two buttons, an
     install bar, a link and a terminal in a viewport a third the height, and
     the desktop rhythm spends most of it on air. */
  @media (max-width: 720px) {
    h1 {
      font-size: clamp(2rem, 8.8vw, 2.75rem);
      letter-spacing: -0.03em;
      max-width: 14ch;
      margin-top: 26px;
    }

    .sub {
      font-size: 0.9375rem;
      line-height: 1.5;
      margin-top: 14px;
    }

    .cta {
      margin-top: 24px;
    }

    .proof {
      margin-top: 26px;
    }

    .demo {
      margin-top: 28px;
    }
  }

  /* ── Scaling the phone stack to the fold.

     The hero has to seat chip, headline, subhead, buttons, install bar,
     terminal, stats and the backer strip. It used to hold every step at a flat
     px value, so the stack came to a fixed ~822px whatever the screen had, and
     the strip — last in order and `flex: none` — was simply pushed out: 269px
     under the fold on an iPhone SE, 77px on a 14. It was in practice never
     seen. So every step here is stated against svh and closes up as the screen
     gets shorter, and the terminal scales with it (its own note carries the
     numbers). Reading order is untouched: copy, terminal, stats, logos.

     The maxima are the old flat values and the coefficients reach them at
     ~900svh, so a phone with the room looks exactly as it did. The floors are
     where air stops being compressible without the stack reading as collapsed.

     What this does and does not buy, measured at each phone's *small* viewport
     (100svh resolves to the toolbars-shown height, so these are well under the
     device heights — an SE is 553, not 667):

       iPhone 14      750   strip in fold
       iPhone 15 Pro  758   strip in fold
       Pixel 7        823   strip in fold
       15 Pro Max     838   strip in fold, terminal still at a full 8px
       iPhone SE      553   still 150px under — see below
       iPhone 13 mini 629   still 86px under

     The last two are arithmetic, not tuning. At 375 the incompressible core is
     394px — 56 nav clearance, 28 chip, 69 headline, 68 subhead, 92 of buttons
     and install bar (44px tap targets, which do not scale), 21 stats, 61 strip
     — and the terminal at its 7px legibility floor is 228 more. That is 622px
     before a single pixel of spacing, against 553 available. No amount of air
     closes a 69px gap that exists with zero air in it. Seating the strip on
     those two screens needs the terminal out of the fold, which is a change to
     reading order and deliberately not made here. They scroll, as they did.

     Re-measure the whole table before touching a coefficient; the fold is won
     or lost in tens of pixels and every number above is measured. ── */
  @media (max-width: 560px) {
    /* Clear the bar, then up to 15px, which is what the old flat 84 was against
       the 69px bar. Stated against --nav-h so the phone's shorter bar actually
       buys the fold the height it saves rather than leaving dead space, and the
       gap term scales with the screen like every other step in the stack — 15
       is now the ceiling rather than a constant.

       The 100svh floor above is left alone. It is a floor, not a height: the
       screens that still cannot seat the stack (see the fit note above) run
       past it and scroll, and the ones that can are unaffected by it. */
    .hero {
      padding-block: calc(var(--nav-h) + clamp(8px, 1.67svh, 15px))
        clamp(18px, 4.44svh, 40px);
    }

    h1 {
      margin-top: clamp(9px, 2svh, 18px);
    }

    .sub {
      margin-top: clamp(6px, 1.33svh, 12px);
    }

    .cta {
      margin-top: clamp(10px, 2.22svh, 20px);
    }

    .demo {
      margin-top: clamp(12px, 2.67svh, 24px);
    }

    .proof {
      margin-top: clamp(9px, 2.22svh, 20px);
    }

    /* Narrower here than the type alone would make it. At 12.5px the message
       ran the chip to within a few pixels of the column on a 375 screen, so it
       read as a full-width banner rather than as a chip — the one element above
       the headline, and the widest thing in the fold. Taking the type, the
       height and the side padding down a step each leaves it sized to its own
       content with the column visible either side of it, which is the shape
       that says "small notice" rather than "bar".
       The ellipsis stays as the backstop it always was, for the narrow screens
       where even this does not fit. */
    .chip {
      max-width: 100%;
      height: 28px;
      font-size: 0.6875rem; /* 11px */
    }

    .chip-text {
      padding: 0 8px;
      gap: 6px;
      min-width: 0;
      overflow: hidden;
      text-overflow: ellipsis;
      white-space: nowrap;
      display: inline-flex;
    }
  }

  /* The pair stays on one row here rather than stacking. What made it not fit
     was never the labels: it was 20px of side padding and a 44px arrow cap, both
     desktop measures that cost more than they buy at this width. Spending those
     instead of the type keeps "Start building" on one line — it wraps inside a
     44px-tall button, and the button clips it — while the labels stay at the
     14.5px they are everywhere else.
     Measured at 360, 375, 390, 412 and 430: one line, no clipping, in both.
     Keyed to 560, the width the rest of the site calls a phone, and not to the
     400 the stack used to sit at: between 400 and ~425 the untightened pair does
     not fit either, which put a 412 Pixel in the gap. */
  @media (max-width: 560px) {
    .actions {
      gap: 8px;
    }

    .primary .label {
      padding: 0 10px;
    }

    .primary .tip {
      width: 34px;
    }

    .secondary {
      padding: 0 10px;
    }
  }

  /* Below 360 the labels genuinely do not fit beside each other at a size worth
     reading, so this is where they stack. Under every phone in the audit, and
     under the 360 Android width the step above is drawn to hold. */
  @media (max-width: 359.98px) {
    .actions {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
  }

  @media (max-width: 420px) {
    .stats .sep {
      display: none;
    }
  }
</style>
